#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# dataset

#% text
# `dataset([row1, row2, ...])` gives the table whose rows are the associations `row1`, `row2`, ..., with a column for every key.

#% text
# `dataset(assoc(c1 -> col1, c2 -> col2, ...))` gives the table whose columns `c1`, `c2`, ... hold the lists `col1`, `col2`, ... of equal length.

#% text
# `dataset([[c1, c2, ...], [v1, v2, ...], ...])` gives the table whose first row names the columns.

#% text
# `dataset(rows, [c1, c2, ...])` gives the table of the lists `rows` under the column names `c1`, `c2`, ....

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A dataset is the term `dataset(assoc(c1 -> col1, ...))`: an association from column names to columns, all of the same length. Columns of machine integers or reals are stored as packed arrays.

#% item
# A key that some rows lack, and the end of a short row, become `missing()` in their column.

#% item
# The list functions `length`, `part`, `first`, `last`, `rest`, `most`, `take`, `drop`, `reverse`, `join`, `select` and `sort_by` work on the rows of a dataset; a row is an association from column names to values.

#% item
# `ds[i]` is row `i`, `ds[i, c]` the value of column `c` in that row, `ds[c]` the whole column `c` when `c` is not an integer, and `ds[i:j]` the dataset of rows `i` to `j`.

#% item
# A dataset is shown as a table: numbers aligned right, strings without quotes, at most 20 rows (the first and the last ones) and a line with its size.

#% item
# `import_data(file, "dataset")` reads a CSV, TSV, whitespace or JSON file as a dataset, and `export_data` writes one back.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A dataset from rows; Lea has no team, so that cell is missing:

#% cell
crew = dataset([assoc("name" -> "Ida", "team" -> "red", "laps" -> 12), assoc("name" -> "Lea", "laps" -> 9), assoc("name" -> "Max", "team" -> "blue", "laps" -> 15)])
crew
#% output
#= name | team    | laps
#= -----+---------+-----
#= Ida  | red     |   12
#= Lea  | missing |    9
#= Max  | blue    |   15
#= dataset: 3 rows, 3 columns

#% text
# The second row, and one of its values:

#% cell
crew[2]
#% output
#= assoc("name" -> "Lea", "team" -> missing(), "laps" -> 9)

#% cell
crew[3, "laps"]
#% output
#= 15

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# From columns:

#% cell
dataset(assoc("x" -> [1, 2, 3], "square" -> [1, 4, 9]))
#% output
#=   x | square
#= ----+-------
#=   1 |      1
#=   2 |      4
#=   3 |      9
#= dataset: 3 rows, 2 columns

#% text
# From a table whose first row is the header:

#% cell
dataset([["planet", "moons"], ["Mars", 2], ["Jupiter", 95]])
#% output
#= planet  | moons
#= --------+------
#= Mars    |     2
#= Jupiter |    95
#= dataset: 2 rows, 2 columns

#% text
# Rows and columns behave like the elements of a list:

#% cell
crew = dataset([assoc("name" -> "Ida", "laps" -> 12), assoc("name" -> "Lea", "laps" -> 9), assoc("name" -> "Max", "laps" -> 15)])
select(crew, r => r["laps"] > 10)
#% output
#= name | laps
#= -----+-----
#= Ida  |   12
#= Max  |   15
#= dataset: 2 rows, 2 columns

#% cell
sort_by(crew, "laps")
#% output
#= name | laps
#= -----+-----
#= Lea  |    9
#= Ida  |   12
#= Max  |   15
#= dataset: 3 rows, 2 columns

#% cell
take(crew, -1)
#% output
#= name | laps
#= -----+-----
#= Max  |   15
#= dataset: 1 row, 2 columns

#% text
# An association of rows:

#% cell
dataset(assoc(r1 -> assoc("a" -> 1, "b" -> 2), r2 -> assoc("a" -> 3, "b" -> 4)))
#% output
#=   a |   b
#= ----+----
#=   1 |   2
#=   3 |   4
#= dataset: 2 rows, 2 columns

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A dataset made from its own rows is the same dataset:

#% cell
dataset(rows(crew)) === crew
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The shown table is a view; inside other values a dataset prints as its term:

#% cell
[dataset(assoc("a" -> [1]))]
#% output
#= [dataset(assoc("a" -> [1]))]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Columns of different lengths are refused:

#% cell
dataset(assoc("a" -> [1, 2], "b" -> [3]))
#% output
#! failure(shape_mismatch, dataset)

#% text
# A row longer than the header is too:

#% cell
dataset([["a"], [1, 2]])
#% output
#! failure(shape_mismatch, dataset)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `rows`, `column`, `columns`, `add_column`, `aggregate`, `pivot`, `join_on`, `missing`, `import_data`
